WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:32.260
<v Speaker 1>286 players came to Columbus, OH for the first pro tour of the new season, and the big story on day one was deck diversity. Cards that hadn't been seen in play for five years were everywhere you looked, and each deck you've enjoyed playing at any point since 1997 seems viable. No one seems to have any idea what the best deck is. An extended Right Now and Rock and Goblins were the two most popular decks, but 31 different archetypes showed up in the hands of at least one player. The beat down decks ranged from Goblins to blue-green Madness,</v>

00:00:32.330 --> 00:01:01.860
<v Speaker 1>the good old white weenie with a smattering of affinity thrown in on top. The primary control decks are Psychatog and a couple different flavors of the Rock, but there's also some blue light control with Isochron scepters and even some mono red. A squirrel prison was even spotted in the feature match area. One round combo decks include reanimator Aluren, Life Minds Desire, and something called Cephalad Breakfast. Each deck seems to have its own flaws and its own strengths, so who knows what's going to make the top 8 on Sunday?</v>

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:30.710
<v Speaker 1>Pierre Canali is in first place at the end of day one. The rookie from France finally making his first Pro Tour went eight. No, the only undefeated record on the day. And he did it running an Affinity deck. There were only 8 Affinity decks in the entire field, as most pros concluded there was just too much hate in this extended format. Whether it was maindeck, Pernicious Deed from the Rock or Sideboard, and Meltdowns in every red deck, Only eight players ran it. But Canali thinks he knows something. Nobody else knows. He's 8. No</v>

00:01:31.390 --> 00:02:01.500
<v Speaker 1>champions of Kamigawa had a pretty big impact on this tournament. Samurai of the Pale Curtain and Ismaros seemed to give new life to White Weenie. Maloku the Clouded mirror showed up through the breach was key to Manuel Bevan sneak attack deck. Kokosu the Evening Star replaced Bizarro and a bunch of rock builds. Kiki Jiki broke a bunch of mirrors and goblin decks. Sakura tried the Elder was all over the place. Both Saju sheltered many a spell. Nezumi grave robbers showed up and probably the most talked about Champions</v>

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:17.910
<v Speaker 1>card was Cranial extraction. There was actually kind of a pre tourney overreaction as many players were terrified that this card would wreck the format. Turns out, you know, it's just, it's good, it's relevant, but the format is quite healthy. It's ought to make a great PTQ format.</v>

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:43.700
<v Speaker 1>One thing that's Pro Tour does not have is 4 time Player of the Year Kai Buddha. One day after celebrating his 25th birthday, he put up the worst result of his illustrious career. One in four and a drop with reanimator defending Player of the year Gabriel in the seat will also be watching from the sidelines. His amazing streak of top aides at Constructed events came to a disappointing end, as he could manage only four wins against 4 losses with a rock deck that splashed blue.</v>

00:02:44.850 --> 00:03:01.890
<v Speaker 1>That's the story as Friday ends here at Pro Tour Columbus. All the players that picked up at least 15 points will come back for Day 2, will play all day in the Top 8, will advance to Sunday, where you can watch all the action live on the magicthegathering.com webcast. It'll start 9:00 AM Eastern Time on Sunday.</v>

